    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>Winter joint pain is real, but cold weather does not damage joints.</strong> Large studies disagree on whether temperature itself raises pain: a 2023 meta-analysis of 14 studies found a small negative correlation, while a case-crossover study of about 350 people with knee osteoarthritis found no link to pain flares at all. Moving less is the better-evidenced cause.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The weather effect is modest:</strong> on a damp, windy, low-pressure day, 2,658 people tracked daily had around a 20% higher chance of more pain than on an average day.</li>
        <li><strong>Temperature alone is inconsistent:</strong> the same study found no average association between temperature and pain across the population.</li>
        <li><strong>Activity is the bigger lever:</strong> pooled pedometer data puts summer step counts 10 to 15% above winter ones, and weaker thigh muscles mean a more painful knee.</li>
        <li><strong>Honest limitation:</strong> no trial has ever tested an oral collagen supplement against seasonal or weather-related joint pain, including ours.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

