      <h2 id="why">Why do joints hurt more in winter?</h2>
      <p>Mostly because of what winter does to your behaviour, and partly because of what it does to your nervous system. The cold is not eroding your cartilage. A 2024 narrative review of human studies published between January 2010 and May 2024 concluded that weather acts as a contextual modifier of osteoarthritis symptoms rather than a primary determinant — and that adverse weather is associated with reduced outdoor activity and lower participation in rehabilitation.</p>
      <p>That second half is the part worth acting on. Winter shortens the walk, cancels the morning park routine, and turns a physiotherapy appointment into something you will restart in February. The knee that hurts in December is often a knee that stopped being loaded in November.</p>
      <p>Three mechanisms are plausible and worth separating, because only one of them is yours to control:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>You move less.</strong> A review of pedometer and accelerometer studies reports step counts 10 to 15% higher in summer than in winter, and individual studies show larger drops. The muscles that protect a knee are the ones that lose condition fastest.</li>
        <li><strong>Cold muscles are stiffer.</strong> Cooling tissue measurably increases muscle stiffness — a shear-wave elastography study of air-pulsed cryotherapy found exactly that. A stiff calf and a stiff quadriceps change how a joint is loaded before you have taken ten steps.</li>
        <li><strong>Pain perception shifts.</strong> Cold lowers the threshold at which a stimulus registers as painful, and expectation plays a part too: if you believe the weather will hurt, you notice the ache you would otherwise have ignored.</li>
      </ol>

