      <h2 id="collagen">Does a collagen supplement help winter joint pain?</h2>
      <p>There is no evidence that it does, because nobody has studied the question. Not one randomised trial has used seasonal or weather-related joint pain as an endpoint after an oral collagen supplement, and that includes ours. We sell Skelecoll and we are telling you this because the alternative is inventing a result.</p>
      <p>What has been studied is collagen for joint pain in general, over months rather than across a cold snap, and <a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">we have set out what those trials found and where they are weak</a>. If you already take a joint supplement, winter is not a reason to expect more from it or to stop.</p>
      <p>Two honest notes about our own label. Skelecoll contains <strong>600IU of Vitamin D3</strong> per shot. That is a maintenance-level amount: it is below the 800IU daily dose that failed to reduce pain in the VIDEO trial, and it will not correct an established deficiency — if your winter 25(OH)D is low, you need a tested, prescribed correction, not a shot. And its <strong>300mg of Glucosamine HCl</strong> is a supporting co-factor, well under the doses used in the glucosamine trials, which were themselves largely unconvincing. <a href="/pages/supplements-for-knee-pain">We go through that evidence ingredient by ingredient elsewhere.</a></p>

