      <h2 id="doctor">When winter joint pain is not the weather</h2>
      <p>Seasonal aching that eases once you move is one thing. Several patterns are not weather at all, and blaming December delays the diagnosis that matters.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Morning stiffness lasting more than 30 to 60 minutes,</strong> especially in the small joints of both hands and worse symmetrically. Prolonged inflammatory stiffness points towards rheumatoid arthritis and needs blood tests, not a heat pad.</li>
        <li><strong>A single hot, red, swollen joint,</strong> particularly the big toe or the knee, coming on over hours. That is an acute pattern — gout and joint infection both present this way — and it is an urgent assessment, not a seasonal flare.</li>
        <li><strong>A joint that is swollen rather than just sore,</strong> or that locks, gives way, or will not take your weight.</li>
        <li><strong>Fever, unexplained weight loss, a rash, or pain that wakes you at night</strong> alongside the joint pain.</li>
        <li><strong>Pain that did not exist last winter and has not settled in six weeks</strong> of sensible movement. New, persistent joint pain deserves an examination regardless of the month, and <a href="/pages/menopause-joint-pain">in women in their forties and fifties there is a separate and commonly missed explanation</a>.</li>
      </ul>

