      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Does cold weather damage your joints?</summary>
          <p>No. No study has shown that cold weather causes or accelerates joint damage. The research on weather and osteoarthritis looks at day-to-day pain and stiffness, and a 2024 narrative review concluded that weather acts as a contextual modifier of symptoms rather than a cause of the disease. What changes in winter is how a joint feels and how much you move it.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is it the cold or the barometric pressure that makes joints ache?</summary>
          <p>The evidence points more towards humidity and air pressure than towards temperature, which is the opposite of what most winter articles say. In the 2,658-person smartphone study published in npj Digital Medicine in 2019, higher humidity, higher wind speed and lower pressure were linked to more pain, while temperature showed no association when averaged across the population. A 2023 meta-analysis of 14 studies did find a small negative correlation with temperature, so the field is genuinely unsettled.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Why do my knees hurt more in a Delhi winter than the temperature seems to justify?</summary>
          <p>Because the exposure is indoor and continuous rather than brief. Indian homes very rarely have central heating, so you spend the whole cold season at ambient temperature instead of escaping it, and outdoor walking routines stop at the same time. Vitamin D also falls sharply over an Indian winter, which matters for bone health even though correcting it does not reliably reduce joint pain.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should I stop exercising when my joints hurt in the cold?</summary>
          <p>No, and this is the most important thing in the article. Reduced activity is the best-evidenced reason winter joints feel worse, and exercise is the intervention with the strongest trial support for knee osteoarthritis. Move the session indoors, warm up for ten minutes rather than two, and reduce the intensity rather than skipping it.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does a hot water bottle or heat pad actually help arthritis pain?</summary>
          <p>It helps comfort, and that is worth having, but do not expect more. The Cochrane review of thermotherapy for knee osteoarthritis found the available trials small and of low quality, with no firm conclusions possible, and its one heat-specific result was that hot packs did not help swelling. A separate Cochrane review found add-on therapies alongside exercise gave no meaningful benefit over sham add-ons alongside exercise.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does taking collagen help with winter joint pain specifically?</summary>
          <p>There is no evidence either way, because no randomised trial has ever used seasonal or weather-related joint pain as an endpoint after an oral collagen supplement. That includes Skelecoll. Collagen has been studied for joint pain over months rather than across a cold snap, and the honest position is that winter is neither a reason to expect more from a supplement nor a reason to stop one.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should I take extra vitamin D in winter for my joints?</summary>
          <p>Get tested, correct a deficiency for your bones, and do not expect it to ease joint pain. Vitamin D falls steeply over an Indian winter and midday winter sun does not reliably raise it. But in the VIDEO trial, 474 people with knee osteoarthritis taking 800IU daily for three years saw no reduction in pain, stiffness or function, and no slowing of joint space narrowing.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>When should I see a doctor about joint pain that started in winter?</summary>
          <p>See a doctor if morning stiffness lasts more than 30 to 60 minutes, if a single joint becomes hot, red and swollen over hours, if a joint swells, locks or will not take your weight, or if there is fever, weight loss, a rash or night pain alongside it. New joint pain that has not settled after six weeks of sensible movement deserves examination whatever the season.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

